{"id":"2f42ee6f-4ee0-4fd8-9806-3e83f758be07","object":"chat.completion","created":1784817322,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\\n    # Validation: ensure all required fields exist\\n    required_fields = ['id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility']\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        for field in required_fields:\\n            if field not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Missing field {field} in node')\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(pid, []).append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # Sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Check for any retained children\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for cid in children_map.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid in retained:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # Tentative removal: check all required symbols still present\\n        # We only remove leaf non-required, so this check is safe but explicit\\n        symbols_after = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid != nid:\\n                symbols_after.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\\n        if not all(rsym in symbols_after for rsym in required_symbols):\\n            continue\\n        # Perform removal\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    # Return sorted retained IDs and removal order\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\\n\"}\n\nExplanation of the code: It validates each node has required fields, builds a children map, iterates over nodes sorted by depth descending then ID ascending, and removes nodes that are not root, not required symbol, removable, have no retained children, and after removal all required symbols still exist. The retained set is updated and removal order recorded. Finally returns sorted retained IDs and removal order.","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that solves the SE-PE-01 task. The description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we don't have actual grammar or interestingness oracle; the fixture shows nodes with IDs, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The required_symbols list specifies symbols that must remain. The goal is to remove removable nodes (tentatively) if all required symbols remain represented. Deeper nodes before shallower. Use node ID as tie-break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nThe expected output? Probably a dict with \"retained_ids\" sorted and \"removal_order\" list of IDs removed in order.\n\nWe need to implement a reduction algorithm: start with all nodes. For each node in order (deep to shallow, then by ID for ties), if it's removable and not required symbol and not root, tentatively remove it (i.e., check if all required symbols are still present in the tree after removal). If yes, remove it. Continue until no more removals? Or just one pass? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we should try to remove as many as possible, but we must consider that removing a node might affect ancestors? Actually, we are reducing the tree by deleting nodes. The tree structure: each node has parent. If we remove a node, its children are also removed? Or do we reattach children? The description says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the fixture only shows nodes with parent relationships. Likely it's about deleting nodes, and if a node is removed, its children are also removed because they become orphaned? Or maybe we need to replace a node with its children? The term \"replacement candidates\" suggests we might replace a node with its subtree. However, the problem statement says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That last part: \"a node with retained children\" – that implies we cannot remove a node if its children are being retained. But if we remove a node, its children would not be retained (since parent removed). So we must check that after removal, the children are also removed? Or we must not remove a node that has children that are themselves retained (i.e., not removed). This is ambiguous.\n\nLet's parse the instructions more carefully:\n\n\"Implement `grammar_guided_node_reduction` through `solve(case)` for this bounded objective: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\n\nStay inside this mechanism boundary: Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded. Validate every required field before computing a result.\n\nConsider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\n\nNever remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\n\nTentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\n\nUse node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\n\nReturn sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\n\nTreat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\"\n\nKey points:\n- \"grammar-valid deletion\" – likely means we can only delete nodes that are \"removable\" per the input.\n- \"replacement candidates\" – maybe we can replace a node with one of its children? But not specified.\n- \"interestingness oracle\" – but not provided; we treat as preserved if all required symbols remain? That might be the only interestingness condition.\n- \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are not being removed (retained), we cannot remove that node. This suggests we can only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose children are also being removed? Actually, if a node has children that are retained, then removing the parent would orphan those children, which is not allowed. So we must remove in a bottom-up manner: we can only remove nodes that have no retained descendants? But the instruction says \"node with retained children\" – children only, not descendants? It says \"children\", so direct children. So if a node has a child that is retained (not removed), we cannot remove that node. So we should process bottom-up: we can remove leaf nodes (if removable and not required, etc.), then maybe their parents become leaves and can be removed later. So we need to iteratively remove leaves that are removable, check required symbols, until no more removals possible.\n\nAdditionally, \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removing the node, check that the set of symbols of all remaining nodes still includes all required_symbols. So we need to track which nodes are retained, and after each tentative removal, check if all required symbols are still present in the retained set.\n\n\"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should consider nodes in order of decreasing depth, and for same depth, by ID (stable tie break).\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build a map of node id to node dict.\n- Build parent-child relationships: for each node, add to children list of parent.\n- Also maintain a set of retained node IDs initially all.\n- Build a list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID ascending (since ID is string, but numeric? In examples, IDs are \"n1\",\"n2\", etc. Lexicographic order? That's fine, but to be safe, maybe sort by integer part? But the fixture shows \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. For tie-break at equal depth, we can use node ID as string. The examples have depth 0 root, depth 1, depth 2. For depth 2 nodes, they have IDs \"n4\",\"n5\",\"n6\". Lexicographic order: n4, n5, n6. That's fine.\n- We'll iterate over nodes in that order. For each node:\n   - Skip if not in retained set (already removed).\n   - Check if node id is root? root is \"root\" (the only node with parent null). We never remove root.\n   - Check if node's symbol is in required_symbols? If yes, skip.\n   - Check if node is removable (removable field) - if not, skip.\n   - Check if node has any retained children: after some removals, children may have been removed. So we need to check the current children list (only those still retained). If any child is retained, skip (cannot remove).\n   - Tentatively remove: check that if we remove this node, all required symbols are still present in the retained set. This requires simulating removal: temporarily remove it from retained set, check required symbols, then if all present, we can actually remove it. If not, keep it.\n   - If allowed, remove it: update retained set, and record removal order (append node ID).\n   - After removal, the parent's children list changes (but we can just recompute children from retained set when needed, or maintain dynamic children list).\n- After processing all nodes once? But note: when we remove a node, its parent might become a leaf (no retained children) later, but we already processed the parent earlier because we processed deeper first. So we need to re-scan? The instruction says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" This suggests a single pass from deep to shallow. But due to removals, a shallower node might become removable after its deeper children are removed. Since we process deeper first, by the time we get to the parent, its children may have been removed, so it becomes a leaf and can be considered. So a single pass from deep to shallow should suffice, because we only remove nodes that have no retained children at the moment of consideration. Since we go deep first, all children of a node are processed before the node. So after processing all children, the parent's children set will be the ones not removed. So we can remove the parent if all its children were removed. That works.\n\nBut careful: The order \"deeper nodes before shallower\" means we iterate depth descending. For depth 2, we have nodes n4,n5,n6. For depth 1, n1,n2,n3. For depth 0, root. So we process depth 2 first. For n4: its parent is n2. n2 has children n4 (removable) and maybe others? n2's other children? The fixture shows n2's children only n4? Actually, from parent fields: n2 parent root, n4 parent n2. So n2 has child n4. n1 has n6. n3 has n5. So when we process depth 2, we consider n4, n5, n6. For n4, it is removable, not required (symbol s1? required symbols root and s1. n4 symbol s1, so it is a required symbol? required_symbols includes \"s1\". So n4's symbol is s1, so it is a required symbol. Therefore we cannot remove n4. Similarly n5 symbol s2, not required. n6 symbol s0, not required. So we process n5 and n6. Both are removable? n5 is removable false, so skip. n6 removable true. Check children: none (leaf). Check required symbols after removal: need to ensure root and s1 remain. root is always there, s1 is present in n1 and n4. n1 is retained (depth1, removable false). n4 is retained (depth2, but symbol s1). So s1 remains. So we can remove n6. Remove n6. Then next depth 1: n1, n2, n3. n1: removable false, skip. n2: removable true, symbol s2, not required. It has children? n4 is retained (s1, not removable? n4 is removable true but we didn't remove it because it's required symbol? Actually, we skipped n4 because it's required. So n4 is retained. So n2 has retained child n4 → cannot remove n2. n3: removable false, skip. root: skip. So final retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5? n5 is not removable, so retained. n6 removed. So retained IDs: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: probably alphabetical? \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\". The instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs\" – sorted presumably by ID order, which is string. So [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. Removal order: [\"n6\"].\n\nBut we need to check if this matches expected? The problem says \"the actual removal order\". We must output both.\n\nNow the case payload has \"_case_nonce\" and \"required_symbols\". We need to validate fields: ensure nodes list not empty, each node has required fields, etc. The instruction: \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check presence of 'id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility'. Also root has parent null. etc.\n\nNow implement solve(case). Return a JSON-serializable object. Probably a dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". Also maybe \"case_id\"? Not required. Just the results.\n\nEdge cases: multiple nodes with same symbol? Required symbols list may have duplicates? Probably not.\n\nThe algorithm should be deterministic.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate case payload: check that case is dict with 'payload', etc. Assume input is well-formed as per fixture. For safety, we can assert fields exist.\n\n2. Extract nodes list, required_symbols list.\n\n3. Build dictionary node_map: id -> node dict.\n\n4. Build children map: parent_id -> list of child ids. Initialize empty lists. For each node, if parent is not None, add child id to parent's list.\n\n5. Initialize retained set: all node ids.\n\n6. Create sorted list of nodes by depth descending, then by id (as string). We'll iterate over the nodes in that order.\n\n7. removal_order = [].\n\n8. For each node in sorted list:\n   - if node['id'] not in retained: continue\n   - if node['id'] == 'root': continue (root never removed)\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue\n   - if not node['removable']: continue\n   - check if any retained child: for each child_id in children_map.get(node['id'], []): if child_id in retained: skip node.\n   - tentative removal: check if all required symbols still present. Create a set of symbols of retained nodes excluding this node. If any required symbol missing, skip.\n   - Actually, we need to check that after removal, the set of symbols of all retained nodes includes all required symbols. So compute current symbol set of retained nodes. Remove this node's symbol from that set (if it contributes). Then check if all required symbols are in that set. Since we only removed one node, we can just check if the node's symbol is required? But required symbols may appear multiple times. So we need to ensure that after removal, at least one node with each required symbol remains. So we can count occurrences: count of each required symbol in retained nodes. If the node's symbol is in required_symbols and its removal would reduce count to zero, then disallow. But we already skip required symbols nodes. So if node is not required symbol, its removal does not affect required symbols directly. However, required symbols could be present only in nodes that are children of this node? But if this node is not required symbol, it doesn't matter. But consider: required symbol s1 appears in nodes n1 and n4. Removing n2 (which is not s1) doesn't affect. So condition \"only if all required symbols remain represented\" after removal is automatically satisfied if we never remove nodes with required symbol? Actually, we could remove a node that is not required symbol but that node might be the only ancestor that contains a required symbol? No, required symbol is a property of the node itself (the symbol field). So if we remove a node that is not required symbol, it doesn't remove any required symbol. However, if a required symbol node is a child of this node, and we remove this node, then the child becomes orphaned? But we already forbid removing nodes with retained children. So if the required symbol node is retained, we cannot remove its parent. So that's handled. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically true if we only remove non-required nodes and ensure no required symbol nodes are removed. But what about the case where a required symbol appears only in nodes that are children of the node we are removing? Those children would be removed as well because we are deleting the subtree? Actually, our algorithm only removes the node itself, not its children. But if we remove a node, its children become disconnected from the tree? The problem doesn't specify what happens to children. The Perses algorithm typically replaces a node with its children (i.e., flattens). But here we have \"deletion and replacement candidates\". The instruction \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" suggests that removing a node might also remove its subtree (because the node is gone). In a tree, if you delete a node, its children are also deleted unless you reattach them. The phrase \"node with retained children\" implies that we cannot remove a node if its children are retained (i.e., we want to keep them). So if we remove a node, its children are not retained; they are implicitly removed as well. Therefore, when we tentatively remove a node, we must consider that all descendants are also removed. The condition \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests we can only remove nodes whose children are also being removed (or have already been removed). So effectively, we remove entire subtrees. Thus, we need to remove a node and all its retained descendants at once? Or we remove node only if it has no retained children (i.e., its children have already been removed). That matches the bottom-up approach: we can only remove leaf nodes. Because if a node has any retained child, we cannot remove it. So the removal of a node implicitly removes that node along with any descendants that are not already removed? But since we only remove leaf nodes, the removal of a leaf node does not affect other nodes. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" after removing a leaf node is just checking that the leaf node itself is not required symbol (which we already skip). So it becomes trivial. But the fixture includes nodes with removable true and symbol not required, so they should be removed if leaf.\n\nBut the instruction also says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This could be a more general condition: after removing the node and its entire subtree, check required symbols. But we already enforce that we don't remove nodes with retained children, so the subtree is already empty of retained children. So it's the same.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll implement the straightforward interpretation: process nodes depth-first, remove only if leaf (no retained children), not root, not required symbol, removable, and check that after removal, all required symbols still present (though this will always be true if we don't remove required symbols). We'll still do the check for safety.\n\nWe also need to output sorted retained IDs. Sorting by ID string ascending.\n\nReturn a dict {\"retained_ids\": list, \"removal_order\": list}.\n\nNow, write the Python code.\n\nI must produce one JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string.\n\nBe careful with escaping: newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nI'll write the code inside a string, then construct the final JSON.\n\nLet's craft the implementation."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1737,"completion_tokens":4406,"total_tokens":6143,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3809},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":73},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}